    "durationMs": 336681,
    "finalText": "Implementation complete. I've built a persistent URL shortener on NestJS 11.2.11 + TypeScript 5.8.3 with TypeORM and PostgreSQL, verified end-to-end with a 73-check test suite plus concurrency and restart tests.\n\n**What's in place:**\n- **Endpoints**: `POST /links` (201 with generated or custom 4–20 char code, 409 on duplicates, 422 on bad input), `GET /links/{code}`, `GET /r/{code}` (atomic 302 redirect with exact `Location`, single `UPDATE … clicks + 1`), `GET /links` (creation-ordered `items` + `total`). All errors are JSON `{error}` — validation/malformed-JSON map to 422, unknown codes to 404.\n- **Persistence**: `links` table with unique `code`, created via a TypeORM migration; `DATABASE_URL` read from the env (never hard-coded); clicks verified to survive restarts (3 → 6 across a server restart) and stay exact under 10 concurrent redirect hits; duplicate-code races resolve to exactly one 201 and the rest 409.\n- **`start.sh`**: builds TypeScript, applies pending migrations (`typeorm migration:run`, idempotent), then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000` — tested from the project root and from `/tmp`.\n- **README** documents prerequisites, `DATABASE_URL`, and the `sh start.sh` flow.\n\nOne quirk worth noting: this environment sets `NODE_ENV=production`, so npm silently omits devDependencies — I installed the `@types/*` packages as regular dependencies to keep the build reproducible. The database was left truncated so the test suite starts from a clean slate."
  }
